48637 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048637 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429500 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048637 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429501 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048637 #edb/ref 1031 "Newton's Method for Optimizing Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048637 #edb/ref 1033 5377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048637 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "b0a48e9b-61a7-525a-a8e3-a080b4dcb7c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1031 "Newton's Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1033 912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050483 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4d42e8ab-7d60-5380-b9d4-046ff9069092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154804 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154804 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154804 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154804 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "784f0dbb-d926-5461-9f9f-98f165e27f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154807 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154807 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154807 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186154807 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "63671e40-590f-536b-8dfb-757855c8f75f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157044 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157044 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^2 + e^{-2x}$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35283781-7e25-5144-9bdd-77921cf5ea26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157044 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}+{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2x-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x-2{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2+4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{2(0.5)-2{e}^{-2(0.5)}}{2+4{e}^{-2(0.5)}} \\\\  & ≈0.424,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157045 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157045 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bf35491-4dda-5bff-9a9e-c3e91f9d5edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157045 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-12x+9, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}-12x+9)^{′}=6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}-12{x}_{n}+9}{6{x}_{n}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}-12{x}_{0}+9}{6{x}_{0}-12} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}-12(0)+9}{6(0)-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}-12{x}_{1}+9}{6{x}_{1}-12} \\\\  & =0.75-\\frac{3(0.75)^{2}-12(0.75)+9}{6(0.75)-12} \\\\  & =0.975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1 = 0.75$ and $x_2 = 0.975.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157046 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157046 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)=x^4-3x+1$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67b4f920-532d-56dc-8b6b-c76d5115959d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157046 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3x+1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-3)^{′}=12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{3}-(4{x}_{n}^{3}-3)}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}_{n}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{8{x}_{0}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(1)^{3}+3}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.916\\,666….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{8{x}_{1}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.916\\,666…)^{3}+3}{12(0.916\\,666…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,631…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{8{x}_{2}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.908\\,631…)^{3}+3}{12(0.908\\,631…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,560….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx0.91$ and $x_3 \\approx 0.91$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x \\approx 0.91,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157426 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157427 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157428 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1045 "Newton's Method for Optimizing Single-Variable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157519 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7117d1d2-5836-549a-9885-24bb3251be4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157713 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157713 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157713 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157713 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157713 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c86499a3-b11b-54e3-8a70-04bd4551cb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157713 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+2x-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}+2x-3)^{′}=6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}-3}{6{x}_{n}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}-3}{6{x}_{0}+2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3}{6(0)+2} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-3}{6{x}_{1}+2} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{3(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)-3}{6(1.5)+2} \\\\  & ≈0.886,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157716 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157716 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157716 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157716 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3}$  in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=-1.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbd4e823-0309-5688-94b6-82a55c915b15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157716 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3})^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+3{x}_{n}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-4{x}_{n}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+3{x}_{0}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-4{x}_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{2(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})+3(-1)^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})-4(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2})+6(-1)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-3}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-6} \\\\  & ≈-0.768,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158355 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158355 #edb/ref 1061 688822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158355 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba6dbaa2-2df5-5b25-ad1e-3045a5009cb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158355 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158356 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158356 #edb/ref 1061 688823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158356 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9795715-d19d-577f-99a4-49bef78578e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158356 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158357 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158357 #edb/ref 1061 688824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a82e30f-f0dc-525a-9d7f-def00a948b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158358 #edb/ref 1061 688827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39b82888-3561-5f52-b687-f896a063cc7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158359 #edb/ref 1061 688830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7207f91-013e-5d36-b69d-1544259c0ba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158438 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to *find the roots* of a function using *Newton’s method*. That is, we solved equations of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0,\n$$\n\nby starting with an initial guess and refining it step by step using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we turn to a closely related problem of *finding a local minimum or maximum* of a function $f(x)$. Recall that these may occur at points where the derivative $f'(x)$ is equal to zero. In other words, we are looking for the roots of the function's derivative.\n\nEarlier, we approximated the function by a straight line near our current guess. Here, instead of using a linear approximation, we assume that the function $f(x)$ behaves like a *parabola* near our current guess. That’s a reasonable assumption if $f$ is smooth. \n\nTo make this idea precise, we use a second-order Taylor approximation, $F(x){:}$\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) + \\dfrac{1}{2} f''(x_n)(x - x_n)^2\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum or maximum, we first need to take the derivative of this approximation, which is\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n).\n$$\n\nWe then need to set $F'(x) =0$:\n\n$$\nf'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n) = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}({x}_{n})(x-{x}_{n}) & =-{f}^{′}({x}_{n}) \\\\ x-{x}_{n} & =-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})} \\\\ x & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, just like in root-finding, we start with an initial guess and improve it step by step. But this time, we apply Newton’s method to the derivative $f'(x)$ instead of the function $f(x)$ itself:\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\n**Note**: The second derivative $f''(x_n)$ must not be zero. If it is zero, or even very close to zero, the update step can become extremely large, leading us far away from the solution.\n\nJust like in root-finding, Newton’s method works best when the function is smooth, and our initial guess is already close to the minimum or maximum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158438 #edb/ref 1065 10738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158438 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0745939b-5be0-5c57-bc01-4f0f87024707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158438 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158457 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\ln(x+1)+\\dfrac{2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nin the interval $[0,2].$ Let's calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.8.$ \n\nThe Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{2}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{4}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-x}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{{x}_{n}-1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}}}{\\frac{3-{x}_{n}}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(0.8)^{2}-1}{3-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.964,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158457 #edb/ref 1065 13141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158457 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8b8920e9-7fa3-56ff-ab8e-2d0283bc3911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158457 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512137 #edb/ref 1080 "x≈1.42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512137 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512137 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11c3f308-2cf6-56a0-b5d5-965d575f5ffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512147 #edb/ref 1080 "0.575" 