 the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.65$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "003098de-0500-522c-8022-2eac5e6b41e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512863 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12x)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}-12)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n})}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.65)^{4}}{20(1.65)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.428\\,269…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.428\\,269…)^{4}}{20(1.428\\,269…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.349\\,003…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}}{20{x}_{2}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.349\\,003…)^{4}}{20(1.349\\,003…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.339\\,016…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{15{x}_{3}^{4}}{20{x}_{3}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.339\\,016…)^{4}}{20(1.339\\,016…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.338\\,865….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.34$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.34$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.34,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512870 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512870 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x$ in the interval $[0.2,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.2.$ Round your answers to three decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0cd7384c-d991-5262-b3c3-37190574fa1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512870 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x)^{′}=\\frac{2}{x}-4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{2}{x}-4)}^{′}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}}-4}{-\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+{x}_{n}(1-2{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}(1-{x}_{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}(1-{x}_{0}) \\\\  & =2(0.2)(1-0.2) \\\\  & =0.32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}(1-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & =2(0.32)(1-0.32) \\\\  & =0.4352\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}=0.32$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.435,$ rounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512874 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512874 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512874 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512874 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12761a27-c492-58e4-90c1-fe69f195f607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512874 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =((1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(-1-2x(1-x)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =((2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(4x-2-2x(2{x}^{2}-2x-1)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(2{x}_{n}^{2}-2{x}_{n}-1){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}-2){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(2{x}_{0}^{2}-2{x}_{0}-1){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{0}^{3}+4{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}-2){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(2(1.5)^{2}-2(1.5)-1){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}}{(-4(1.5)^{3}+4(1.5)^{2}+6(1.5)-2){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512884 #edb/ref 1069 "q-367477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512884 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512884 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.2$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34cbee90-4c9c-598a-b567-3653b89bdcd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512884 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4)^{′}={e}^{x}-12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-12{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-12{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{{e}^{1.2}-6(1.2)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.2}-12(1.2)} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-12{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875…-\\frac{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-6(1.080\\,875…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-12(1.080\\,875…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6{x}_{2}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-12{x}_{2}} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…-\\frac{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-6(1.074\\,640…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-12(1.074\\,640…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,623….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.07$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.07$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.07,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512894 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512894 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512894 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512894 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512894 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69e00eda-cc65-5659-9307-298376017360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512894 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3)^{′}=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2)^{′}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3} \\\\  & =1.367\\,897…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =(1.367\\,897…)-\\frac{2(1.367\\,897…)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+(1.367\\,897…)-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+3} \\\\  & ≈1.306,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512896 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512896 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512896 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512896 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x}$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512896 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70144f61-d6d7-503d-8fc3-74d38b71f718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512896 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-2x+2-(-{x}^{2}+2x+1)){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+1){e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}{({x}_{n}^{2}-4{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(-{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}}{({x}_{0}^{2}-4{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(-(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}}{((1.5)^{2}-4(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}} \\\\  & ≈2.136,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512916 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512916 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512916 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512916 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512916 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a34954f0-edee-5e78-b8c6-4b95b31dcf9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512916 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5)^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-4x+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-4x+1)^{′} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4(3{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-4{x}_{n}+1}{4(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-4{x}_{0}+1}{4(3{x}_{0}^{2}-1)} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{4(1.8)^{3}-4(1.8)+1}{4(3(1.8)^{2}-1)} \\\\  & ≈1.309,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-367472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512924 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afb3acd5-a2e1-5607-b3f9-fd70e360b05d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512924 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x)^{′}=5{x}^{4}+6x-1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}+6x-1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}+6)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1)}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.5)^{4}+1}{20(0.5)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.227\\,941….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.227\\,941…)^{4}+1}{20(0.227\\,941…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,829…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{2}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.166\\,829…)^{4}+1}{20(0.166\\,829…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,033….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.17$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.17$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.17,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512925 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1}$  in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0787255-f508-561f-a5f7-88e7552739fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512925 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{0}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{0}+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{0.8+2}-\\frac{1}{(0.8+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{(0.8+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(0.8+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈0.575,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512935 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512935 #edb/ref 1069 "q-367494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512935 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512935 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,0.5],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512935 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2a1716c-abcd-5c70-b5af-eb300c75e232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512935 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2})^{′}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x=2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3x), \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-6=-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n})}{-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})-3{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =0.5+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)-3\\cdot 0.5}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})-3{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)-3\\cdot 0.272\\,999…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…., \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{2})-3{x}_{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{2})+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)-3\\cdot 0.283\\,140…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,068….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.28$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.28$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.28,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512940 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512940 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2x-1$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce107c95-0185-5fbd-a5eb-aca5a16f428e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512940 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2x-1)^{′}={e}^{x}-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{0.5}-2}{{e}^{0.5}} \\\\  & =0.713\\,061…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =(0.713\\,061…)-\\frac{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}-2}{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}} \\\\  & ≈0.693,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-389801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512948 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.8$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de551d86-8bf0-5d70-b4cf-bdf9b46a682a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512948 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2})^{′}=6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x)^{′}=30{x}^{4}-40{x}^{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2)-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n})}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}-30{x}_{n}^{4}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{5}-15{x}_{n}^{4}}{15{x}_{n}^{4}-20{x}_{n}^{3}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{12{x}_{0}^{5}-15{x}_{0}^{4}}{15{x}_{0}^{4}-20{x}_{0}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.8)^{5}-15(1.8)^{4}}{15(1.8)^{4}-20(1.8)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.656\\,564….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{12{x}_{1}^{5}-15{x}_{1}^{4}}{15{x}_{1}^{4}-20{x}_{1}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.656\\,564…)^{5}-15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}}{15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}-20(1.656\\,564…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.594\\,573…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{12{x}_{2}^{5}-15{x}_{2}^{4}}{15{x}_{2}^{4}-20{x}_{2}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.594\\,573…)^{5}-15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}}{15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}-20(1.594\\,573…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,950…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{12{x}_{3}^{5}-15{x}_{3}^{4}}{15{x}_{3}^{4}-20{x}_{3}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.582\\,950…)^{5}-15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}}{15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}-20(1.582\\,950…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,567….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.58$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.58$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.58,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512956 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6e563d1-d5b4-5654-a9e3-927ced1c53e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512956 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-24{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{5{x}_{0}^{4}-12{x}_{0}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-24{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{5(2)^{4}-12(2)^{2}+1}{20(2)^{3}-24(2)} \\\\  & =1.705\\,357…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{5{x}_{1}^{4}-12{x}_{1}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-24{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.705\\,357…)-\\frac{5(1.705\\,357…)^{4}-12(1.705\\,357…)^{2}+1}{20(1.705\\,357…)^{3}-24(1.705\\,357…)} \\\\  & ≈1.561,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
